package radiant:reconcile@0.1.0;

/// The Perseid step contract as WIT (ADR-0075).
///
/// A step OBSERVES and EMITS; it performs no I/O of its own. Both are imports,
/// so a component that was not granted them DOES NOT INSTANTIATE — the
/// capability check is at link time, not a runtime branch the program could
/// route around. That property is the reason a step is a component at all.
///
/// Observation is THREE-VALUED and the variant enforces it. `absent` means the
/// thing is not there; `unknown` means we could not tell. A host that returned
/// only `option<...>` would collapse them, and a step would conclude "gone,
/// tear down" from an apiserver blip — the most-repeated defect in this
/// codebase's history.
/// The vocabulary both read surfaces share.
///
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// ***`obs` LIVED IN `observe` UNTIL 2026-08-29, AND THAT MADE ONE INTERFACE
/// IMPLY ANOTHER*** (engi: "why observe-cluster and observe can't be unified?"
/// then "do shared interface").
///
/// `observe-cluster` returns the same three-valued observation, so it wrote
/// `use observe.{obs}`. In the component model a TYPE dependency materialises as
/// an INTERFACE IMPORT, so every world importing `observe-cluster` also imported
/// `observe` - measured on the built artifact:
///
///     import radiant:reconcile/observe@0.1.0          <- implied, never called
///     import radiant:reconcile/observe-cluster@0.1.0
///
/// The capability gate compares `spec.capabilities` to the ARTIFACT's imports,
/// so a program that only reads cluster-scoped objects had to be GRANTED the
/// namespaced read it never calls. Not an escalation - `observe.get` is confined
/// by the grant's namespace either way - but a grant nobody can justify from the
/// code, which is precisely what this system says a capability should never be.
///
/// A types-only interface has no functions, so importing it confers nothing.
/// Both read surfaces `use types.{obs}` and neither implies the other.
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
interface types {
  variant obs {
    known(string),
    absent,
    unknown,
  }
}

interface observe {
  use types.{obs};

  /// Read one object by path. Never throws: failure is `unknown`.
  get: func(path: string) -> obs;

  /// Count members of a collection. Never throws: failure is `unknown`.
  ///
  /// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  /// ***THE QUERY IS A LABEL SELECTOR, NOT A PATH. engi decided it 2026-08-21.***
  ///
  ///     count("app=demo")                                    YES
  ///     count("/api/v1/namespaces/n/pods?labelSelector=..")  NO
  ///
  /// ***THE UNIT PROBLEM FROM `now()`, ONE FUNCTION LATER, AND IT COST A LIVE
  /// TRACE.*** `query: string` is equally satisfied by a selector and by a path,
  /// so the two halves of this system picked differently and neither was wrong
  /// on its own terms:
  ///
  ///     internal/reconcilehost   labels.Parse  -> a selector
  ///     examples/wasm/...        a path
  ///
  /// A path fails `labels.Parse`, so the count is `unknown`; `count` never
  /// throws by contract, so NOTHING reports it, and the step yields forever
  /// while looking healthy. Measured on a live node: 239 asks answered, 119
  /// resolved — one read per pass resolving and one not. `now()`'s own doc
  /// twenty lines up predicted exactly this: *"the same disagreement found
  /// AFTER the second implementation is a silent wrong answer in production
  /// with no failing test to lead anyone to it."*
  ///
  /// # WHY A SELECTOR AND NOT A PATH, WHICH IS THE READING `get` WOULD SUGGEST
  ///
  /// ***A SELECTOR CANNOT NAME A NAMESPACE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.*** The scope
  /// of a count is the GRANT's namespace, supplied by the host. A path lets a
  /// program NAME one — which then has to be checked against the grant, so the
  /// boundary gains a second enforcement point that can disagree with the first.
  ///
  /// That trade is already recorded in this tree, made the other way and
  /// regretted: `perseidrun/assemble.go` notes that `ScalePatcher` took NO
  /// namespace argument *"deliberately, because that is what makes the boundary
  /// unforgeable from a program's side"*, and that replacing it with a
  /// per-call namespace was *"strictly weaker — the replacement rests on a
  /// DERIVATION staying correct rather than on a capability being absent."*
  ///
  /// A selector keeps `count` in the strong form: there is nothing to bind
  /// wrongly because there is nothing to bind. `get` takes a path because it
  /// names ONE object and the name is the whole argument; the namespace segment
  /// there is checked and must match. The vocabularies differ because the
  /// questions differ, and that is a decision rather than an accident.
  ///
  /// `apsis aperture explain-count <query>` reports what a host does with a
  /// query, and names a path specifically when it sees one.
  /// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  count: func(query: string) -> obs;

  /// The clock, as a capability — so a replay can supply a recorded one and a
  /// step stays a pure function of its observations.
  ///
  /// EPOCH MILLISECONDS. UTC, since the Unix epoch. engi decided it 2026-08-19.
  ///
  /// THE UNIT IS IN THE CONTRACT BECAUSE IT WAS NOT, AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE
  /// POINT — `-> u64` is equally satisfied by seconds, millis, micros and nanos,
  /// so every implementation picks one and none of them is wrong on its own
  /// terms. Two that pick differently do not fail: `resume.after` arithmetic
  /// comes out off by a factor of 1000 and NOTHING ERRORS, because both numbers
  /// are plausible u64s. A parked program simply wakes at the wrong time, or in
  /// forty years.
  ///
  /// Written down while trail is still the ONLY implementation. cmd/trail's
  /// reconcile.rs pinned millis on 2026-08-18 because somebody had to go first,
  /// and radiant's serving half does not exist yet — so this cost one sentence.
  /// The same disagreement found AFTER the second implementation is a silent
  /// wrong answer in production with no failing test to lead anyone to it.
  ///
  /// Millis rather than nanos so a u64 cannot overflow within any plausible
  /// lifetime, and rather than seconds so a sub-second `resume.after` is
  /// expressible at all.
  now: func() -> u64;
}

/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// `interface emit` AND ITS `act(op: string, args: string)` WERE DELETED
/// 2026-08-21, decided by engi. Actions are TYPED and live in their own
/// interfaces below — see `workloads`.
///
/// WHY, AND IT IS NOT A STYLE CHANGE. A Perseid carried two authority lists:
/// what its component may LINK, and what the host will DO on its behalf. engi
/// asked why those are not one. They could not be, and the reason was `act`
/// taking a STRING: an action's name was a runtime value, so nothing in a
/// component's TYPES named it and no derivation could reach it.
///
/// ***THE GRANT IS AUTHORED AND THE DEMAND IS DERIVED — NOT THE OTHER WAY ROUND,
/// AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS PARAGRAPH HAD IT BACKWARDS*** (caught by
/// trail-main, measured). It said spec.capabilities was "DERIVED from its types
/// by derive-wit", copied from the CRD's own description, which the code
/// contradicts:
///
///     perseid_controller.go   Capabilities: stringsAt(obj,"spec","capabilities")
///                             — AUTHORED in the CR. It is the GRANT.
///     derive-wit              emits `world step-derived { import ... }`
///                             — the DEMAND the artifact makes.
///     trailop/perseid.go      UngrantedCapabilities = demand MINUS grant
///
/// ***ANYONE ACTING ON THE OLD SENTENCE WOULD MAKE THE GRANT SELF-CERTIFYING***
/// — derived from the artifact, so the artifact permits itself, both gates
/// collapse, and the runtime check below becomes vacuous. The two are the same
/// question, which is why the error is worth spelling out rather than deleting.
///
/// The accurate claim is narrower and is what the typing actually bought: the
/// DEMAND and the GRANT are now expressible in the SAME VOCABULARY at the same
/// granularity, so a grant can be checked against a DERIVATION instead of against
/// a hand-written list trusted to be complete. The grant stays INDEPENDENT, and
/// that independence is exactly what keeps the runtime check meaningful.
///
/// ***THAT IS NOT A PRINCIPLE, IT IS A CONSEQUENCE OF A STRINGLY-TYPED SEAM***,
/// and it contradicted what this system says it is. examples/wasm/perseid-join.sh:
/// "a capability nobody supplies is not a permission it lacks — it is a function
/// that does not exist." True for observe and emit as interfaces, and FALSE for
/// every action reachable through one string argument.
///
/// Typed: ONE list, derived from the artifact, and an ungranted action does not
/// instantiate. The gate moves from apply time to LINK time.
///
/// ***THE RUNTIME CHECK IS STILL THERE AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANCY.*** Link-time
/// gating binds an HONEST component. Radiant receives obligations over QUIC and
/// cannot see what a pod linked, so a compromised one can send anything. Radiant
/// re-checks per call — against the SAME list, spec.capabilities, rather than a
/// second vocabulary. The merge holds; defence in depth survives it.
///
/// (op, args) SURVIVES AS THE HOST'S INTERNAL IDENTITY. The ledger still dedups
/// on it and the tracker still retires by non-reassertion; the shim converts a
/// typed call into that pair. Nothing below the guest boundary changed.
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

/// Actions over workloads. Importing this interface IS the authority to perform
/// them — there is nothing else to grant and nothing to check at link time.
///
/// Emitting an obligation DECLARES that something should be true. It does not
/// perform it: the runtime services obligations, deduplicates them by identity,
/// and owns back-off. A step never records that it asked — it re-derives the
/// same conclusion from the same observation next tick, which is why in-flight
/// bookkeeping cannot leak from a program.
///
/// ⛔ ***DEPRECATED (engi, 2026-08-31: "we deprecated workloads.scale and
/// status.set" / "okay status can stay"). USE `ensure` INSTEAD.***
///
/// `scale(path, replicas)` renders `Ensure(path, "spec.replicas", n)` — exactly
/// what `ensure(path, "spec.replicas", num(n))` renders. Same text, same ledger
/// key, same boundary. The specialized interface predates the general one and
/// buys nothing now it exists; it is the write-side twin of the read-side
/// collapse in `internal/aperture/get.go` ("write generalized read — deprecate
/// specialized reads").
///
/// ***`status` IS NOT DEPRECATED WITH IT, AND THE TWO ARE NOT A PAIR.***
/// `SetCondition` is `Self: true` — it takes NO path and writes the program's
/// OWN Perseid status, so a program cannot name another object's conditions.
/// An `ensure`-based replacement would have to accept a path, which gives that
/// property away, and the host owns `lastTransitionTime` through a
/// read-modify-write no obligation expresses. So it stays.
///
/// ***STILL LINKED AND STILL CORRECT.*** Deprecated is not removed: live
/// components on the fleet import this, and a world's imports are what the host
/// supplies, so dropping it strands every one of them until rebuilt.
///
/// ⚠ ***AND ITS NARROWER GRANT IS THE ONE REASON TO KEEP REACHING FOR IT.***
/// `internal/aperture/effects.go` scopes this interface to the single field
/// `spec.replicas`; `ensure` writes ANY field. A program that only ever scales
/// is strictly better bounded holding this capability than the general one, so
/// migrating WIDENS its authority unless the narrowing is replaced by something.
/// "Deprecated" here means the general interface exists — not that this grant
/// is obsolete.
interface workloads {
  /// Scale a workload, named by its apiserver path.
  ///
  /// THE PATH IS THE SAME VOCABULARY spec.writes USES, so the write boundary and
  /// the effect target are one language rather than two dialects that agree by
  /// accident.
  ///
  /// ***READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A SECOND FUNCTION HERE*** (trail-main). Authority
  /// is conferred by IMPORTING THE INTERFACE, so a second function in this
  /// interface is granted to every program that was granted `scale` — one import,
  /// every verb in it. That is fine while the interface holds one action and is a
  /// silent widening the moment it holds two.
  ///
  /// A verb with a different blast radius belongs in its OWN interface, so a
  /// grant can name it separately. If it genuinely belongs beside `scale`, split
  /// the wire gate first: perseidrun's askCapability and aperture's `performs`
  /// both key on the action name, and only the LINK gate is interface-shaped.
  ///
  /// IT RETURNS NOTHING ON PURPOSE, AND THIS IS THE FIRST QUESTION ANYONE
  /// IMPLEMENTING THE HOST ASKS. Its neighbour in periapsis:component has an
  /// error channel - `emit: func(entry) -> result<_, log-error>` - so the
  /// LOGGING interface reports failure and the OBLIGATION interface does not,
  /// which reads backwards.
  ///
  /// It is not. A result here would hand the step a failure it MUST NOT HANDLE:
  /// handling means remembering, remembering means in-flight bookkeeping inside
  /// the program, and that is the leak the paragraph above forbids. A step that
  /// can observe its own obligation failing is no longer TOTAL.
  ///
  /// THE LEVEL-TRIGGERED PROPERTY IS THE ERROR CHANNEL: the obligation fails,
  /// the world does not change, and the step re-derives the same conclusion from
  /// the same observation and re-emits. Nothing is lost because nothing was
  /// remembered.
  ///
  /// THE HAZARD THIS LEAVES IS THE HOST'S, NOT THE GUEST'S, and it is why a
  /// serving implementation needs a DROPPED-OBLIGATION COUNTER before it needs
  /// anything else. Re-derivation only rescues a program that is RE-ENTERED. A
  /// step that emits an obligation and then quiesces on a condition that
  /// obligation was supposed to cause parks forever if the host drops it - and
  /// the guest cannot see that by contract, because `scale` returns nothing and
  /// the resume expression is evaluated by the host WITHOUT running the step.
  ///
  /// That is a program correctly asleep on a condition nobody will satisfy,
  /// which is indistinguishable from a program correctly asleep. ADR-0075's
  /// invariant - "correctly asleep is an inspectable state rather than something
  /// indistinguishable from wedged" - is at risk from the HOST side, and the
  /// remedy is a signal to the OPERATOR rather than an error to the guest.
  scale: func(path: string, replicas: s32);
}

/// Reporting a conclusion about the step's OWN Perseid.
///
/// ITS OWN INTERFACE RATHER THAN A SECOND FUNCTION IN `workloads`. engi decided
/// it 2026-08-25, and it is the rule written twenty lines up being applied the
/// first time it had a customer: authority is conferred by IMPORTING an
/// interface, so `set` beside `scale` would grant every scaling program the
/// ability to write status, and every reporting program the ability to scale.
/// Separate interfaces are what make those two grants nameable apart.
///
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// ***THERE IS NO PATH ARGUMENT, AND THAT IS THE POINT.*** engi decided it
/// 2026-08-25. The subject is the GRANT's perseid, supplied by the host.
///
/// This is `count`'s reasoning, not `scale`'s, and the difference is which
/// question is being asked. `scale` names ANOTHER object, so the name is the
/// whole argument and must be checked against the write boundary. A status
/// conclusion is about the reporter itself — there is no second object to name,
/// so a path argument would exist only to be validated back to the one value it
/// is allowed to hold. That is the trade `perseidrun/assemble.go` records as
/// made once and regretted: *"strictly weaker — the replacement rests on a
/// DERIVATION staying correct rather than on a capability being absent."*
///
/// A step cannot report on another Perseid because there is nothing to bind
/// wrongly, not because something refuses it.
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
interface status {
  /// Three-valued, and it is Kubernetes' own `ConditionStatus`.
  ///
  /// `unknown` is a REPORT OF NOT KNOWING, not a failure to report — the same
  /// discipline `obs` enforces at the top of this file, arriving on the write
  /// side. A two-valued status would make a step collapse "I checked and it is
  /// false" into "I could not tell", which is the defect `obs` exists to
  /// prevent, mirrored.
  enum condition-status {
    true,
    false,
    unknown,
  }

  /// A Kubernetes condition. engi decided the vocabulary 2026-08-25: *copy
  /// Kubernetes* — so an operator reads one language across our objects and
  /// theirs, and `kubectl wait --for=condition=...` works without a translation
  /// layer nobody would maintain.
  ///
  /// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  /// ***THIS RECORD IS `metav1.Condition` MINUS TWO FIELDS, AND WHICH TWO IS
  /// DERIVED RATHER THAN DECIDED*** (trail-main, marked as derived because the
  /// vocabulary is engi's and this split is not). The canonical struct in
  /// `k8s.io/apimachinery@v0.37.0-rc.0` has six fields; a guest supplies four.
  ///
  ///     type · status · reason · message      THE GUEST'S — its conclusion
  ///     lastTransitionTime                    THE HOST'S — see below
  ///     observedGeneration                    THE HOST'S — see below
  ///
  /// ***`lastTransitionTime` IS NOT OMITTED FOR TIDINESS — A GUEST CANNOT
  /// COMPUTE IT WITHOUT BECOMING NON-TOTAL.*** Kubernetes defines it as the last
  /// time the condition transitioned FROM ONE STATUS TO ANOTHER, so producing it
  /// requires the PREVIOUS condition. A step that held the previous condition
  /// would be doing in-flight bookkeeping inside the program — precisely the
  /// leak `workloads` above forbids, arriving by a different door. The host has
  /// the old object in hand and compares; the guest re-derives its conclusion
  /// from its observations and does not remember having reported it.
  ///
  /// A step DOES import `observe.now()`, so it could stamp a timestamp. That is
  /// the trap: it would be a WELL-FORMED WRONG ANSWER — a transition time that
  /// advances on every tick whether or not anything transitioned, and nothing
  /// would error.
  ///
  /// `observedGeneration` is the host's for the ordinary reason: it is a fact
  /// about the object being written, which the guest cannot see and must not
  /// guess. A stale value here makes a current-looking condition report on a
  /// spec nobody is running.
  /// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  record condition {
    /// CamelCase, and the identity of the condition — a second `set` with the
    /// same type REPLACES rather than appends, exactly as
    /// `meta.SetStatusCondition` does.
    %type: string,
    status: condition-status,
    /// CamelCase, machine-readable. Kubernetes requires it non-empty.
    reason: string,
    /// Human-readable. Kubernetes requires it non-empty.
    message: string,
  }

  /// IT RETURNS NOTHING, FOR `scale`'S REASON AND NOT A NEW ONE. A result would
  /// hand the step a failure it must not handle: handling means remembering, and
  /// a step that can observe its own report failing is no longer TOTAL. The
  /// level-triggered property is the error channel here too — the write fails,
  /// the world does not change, and the next pass re-derives the same conclusion
  /// from the same observations and re-reports it.
  ///
  /// The host-side hazard `scale` documents applies unchanged: a dropped report
  /// is invisible to the guest by contract, so it is a signal the OPERATOR needs
  /// and not an error the guest can be given.
  set: func(c: condition);
}

/// Reading an object that lives OUTSIDE any namespace.
///
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// ***ITS OWN INTERFACE, BECAUSE THE NAMESPACE GRANT CANNOT CONFINE IT.*** engi
/// decided the authority model 2026-08-29: this interface confers the VERB at
/// link time, and `spec.reads` names WHICH objects. Both halves are required.
///
/// That is the split `workloads` already uses and this file already argues for:
/// importing an interface IS the authority to perform what it declares, and the
/// per-object list is what stops one import becoming a grant over everything.
/// A single wide interface — "may read any cluster-scoped object" — was
/// considered and rejected for exactly that reason.
///
/// ***WHY NOT JUST WIDEN `observe.get`.*** A namespaced read is confined by
/// comparing the path's namespace to the grant's. A cluster-scoped object has no
/// namespace, so that comparison cannot confine it — `reconcilehost.Host.Get`
/// would fall through to a check that is structurally incapable of refusing.
/// Keeping them apart means a program that never imports this interface cannot
/// reach a cluster-scoped object even if it constructs the path: the function
/// does not exist in its world.
///
/// ***IT RETURNS `obs`, SO IT IS THREE-VALUED FOR THE USUAL REASON.*** `absent`
/// is "the object is not there"; `unknown` is "we could not tell". A monitor
/// that collapses them reports a missing policy when the apiserver blipped,
/// which is a false alarm about the fleet's authorisation — and the opposite
/// collapse hides a real one.
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
interface observe-cluster {
  use types.{obs};

  /// Read one cluster-scoped object by its full apiserver path.
  ///
  /// A PATH, not a name: unlike `Replicas`, there is no grant namespace to
  /// resolve a bare name against, so the path is the whole address. It is
  /// checked against `spec.reads` by EXACT match — never by prefix or kind,
  /// which would collapse the per-object grant back into the interface import.
  ///
  /// Never throws: an unreadable object, and one this program was not granted,
  /// are both observations rather than errors. A path outside the grant reports
  /// `absent` — "not in your world" is correctly indistinguishable from "not
  /// there", and `unknown` would invite a retry for a question that will never
  /// be answerable.
  get: func(path: string) -> obs;
}

/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// READING A SECRET, AND IT IS ITS OWN INTERFACE FOR THE REASON
/// `observe-cluster` IS.
///
/// ***A SECRET IS NOT A WORKLOAD WITH DIFFERENT FIELDS - IT IS THE ONE KIND
/// WHOSE VALUE IS THE POINT.*** `observe` confers pods and the workload kinds,
/// where a program reads shape and counts. Folding secrets into it would widen
/// every program that ever wanted a replica count into one that can read every
/// credential in its namespace, and nothing in the manifest would show the
/// difference.
///
/// ***UNTIL 2026-09-01 THERE WAS NO WAY TO GRANT THIS AT ALL.*** `CapSecretsRead`
/// existed in the host and no interface conferred it, so the aperture's per-kind
/// gate refused every secret read and `Host.Get` had no arm to serve one - while
/// two doc comments claimed a program "may read a Secret in its step". This is
/// that claim becoming true.
///
/// ⚠ ***PARKING ON A SECRET LEAKS THROUGH TWO CHANNELS AND ONLY ONE IS
/// CLOSED.*** A resume naming a secret is rendered into `status.waitingFor`,
/// which anyone with `get perseid` can read - that predicate is redacted. The
/// WAKE TIME is not hidden and cannot be: a program that wakes when a value
/// changes necessarily reveals that it changed. What bounds it is that only a
/// program already granted this interface can build such a predicate, and that
/// program could read the secret outright.
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
interface observe-secrets {
  /// ***DELIBERATELY EMPTY: THIS INTERFACE IS A CAPABILITY, NOT A CALL.***
  ///
  /// It had a `get(name)` for one day. The host read collapsed eight `Get*`
  /// symbols into a single `Get` long before that (see internal/aperture/get.go),
  /// and a second name-only route reintroduced exactly what that collapse
  /// deleted - two ways to read one object, and a step that addressed a Secret
  /// differently from the resume beside it.
  ///
  /// A Secret is read like anything else: `observe.get` with the object's path,
  /// which the host gates per KIND against this capability. Naming the interface
  /// in `spec.capabilities` is what confers it; there is nothing to import.
}

/// Reading a ConfigMap, and its own interface for the reason `observe-secrets`
/// is one.
///
/// ***DATA-BEARING, EVEN THOUGH IT IS NOT SECRET.*** A ConfigMap's VALUE is the
/// point of reading one, so it does not belong in `observe` beside replica
/// counts - the same argument, one notch less severe. Splitting it also means a
/// program's manifest says which of the two it needs, which is what lets radiant
/// decide whether to run the informer at all.
interface observe-configmaps {
  /// ***DELIBERATELY EMPTY: THIS INTERFACE IS A CAPABILITY, NOT A CALL.***
  ///
  /// It had a `get(name)` for one day. The host read collapsed eight `Get*`
  /// symbols into a single `Get` long before that (see internal/aperture/get.go),
  /// and a second name-only route reintroduced exactly what that collapse
  /// deleted - two ways to read one object, and a step that addressed a Secret
  /// differently from the resume beside it.
  ///
  /// A Secret is read like anything else: `observe.get` with the object's path,
  /// which the host gates per KIND against this capability. Naming the interface
  /// in `spec.capabilities` is what confers it; there is nothing to import.
}

/// A step: one total pass. Returns its outcome as JSON — `{"o":"yield"}`,
/// `{"o":"quiesce","resume":{...}}` or `{"o":"terminate"}`.
///
/// The resume expression is DATA so the host can evaluate it without running
/// the step, index parked programs by it, and render it into status.waitingFor.
/// WRITE ANY FIELD OF ANY OBJECT THE APERTURE ALLOWS.
///
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// ***THE CAPABILITY EXISTED AND THE INTERFACE DID NOT.***
/// `internal/aperture/ensure.go` has declared `IfaceEnsure =
/// "radiant:reconcile/ensure@0.1.0"` for as long as generalized effects have
/// existed: a Perseid could name it in `spec.capabilities`, the aperture would
/// confer the `Ensure` action, and the applier would perform it. There was no
/// WIT interface with that id, so nothing could ever CALL it.
///
/// A guest's whole write vocabulary was `workloads.scale` and `status.set` -
/// two hard-coded obligation shapes - while the host carried a general
/// `Ensure(path, field, value)` reachable only from a resume expression.
///
/// # WHY THIS IS NOT `workloads` WITH A WIDER ARGUMENT
///
/// `effects.go` scopes `IfaceWorkloads` to the single field `spec.replicas`,
/// and its own comment says adding `ensure` there "would confer" the general
/// write on everything holding the scale capability. The interface IS the
/// grant, so a program that may scale a Deployment must not thereby be able to
/// rewrite its image, its serviceAccountName or its env.
///
/// # THE VALUE IS A VARIANT, AND A STRING WOULD HAVE BEEN A BUG
///
/// The expression language is typed: `Ensure(p, "spec.replicas", 3)` sets a
/// NUMBER and `Ensure(p, "data.x", "3")` sets the four-character string `"3"`.
/// A `string` parameter here would force every value through quoting and make
/// numeric and boolean fields unwritable - silently, since both spellings parse.
/// The variant is what lets the host emit the right literal.
interface ensure {
  /// A field value, typed as the expression language types it.
  variant value {
    /// A quoted string literal. `text("Now()")` writes those five characters;
    /// it does NOT evaluate.
    text(string),
    /// ⚠ ***UNSIGNED, AND IT WAS `s64` FOR ONE COMMIT.*** The host grammar has
    /// NO UNARY MINUS — `grammar.go` excludes it deliberately, on the reasoning
    /// that "negative literals have no use in this language (a replica count, a
    /// millisecond epoch, a length)". That was true when those were the only
    /// values; `ensure` writes ANY field of ANY object, so it is not any more.
    ///
    /// Measured rather than assumed, against the parser:
    ///
    ///	Ensure(p, "spec.replicas", 7)    parses
    ///	Ensure(p, "spec.replicas", -7)   DOES NOT PARSE
    ///
    /// So a signed arm let a guest render an obligation the host cannot read —
    /// failing at APPLY time, in a pod, inside a pass already recorded, with no
    /// channel to tell the guest because an effect returns nothing. Found by
    /// feeding trail's own expected test strings to the Go parser; both sides'
    /// tests passed independently and the pair was broken.
    ///
    /// `u64` makes it unspellable instead. A genuinely negative field value is
    /// therefore unwritable. The remedy if it ever matters is the one that was
    /// taken for booleans the same day: change the GRAMMAR, not this variant.
    %num(u64),
    /// A boolean. See the note below for why this arm existed, vanished and
    /// came back in one day.
    %flag(bool),
  }

  // ***THE BOOLEAN ARM IS BACK, AND THE GRAMMAR IS WHY (engi, 2026-08-31: "can
  // you fix it too").*** It was removed on the same day for being unrenderable:
  //
  //	Ensure(p, "d", true)     DID NOT PARSE
  //	Get(p, "d") != true      DID NOT PARSE either
  //
  // The language had always had boolean VALUES - `.exists` yields one, every
  // comparison yields one, `!` negates one - and no way to write a boolean
  // CONSTANT. Invisible while the only effect was `scale`, whose value is a
  // replica count; `Ensure` writes any field of any object, and `spec.paused` is
  // an ordinary thing to want.
  //
  // `TRUE`/`FALSE` tokens now exist and a boolean is a primary like any other
  // value, so it composes as an effect argument, as a comparison operand and
  // under `!`. Neither `"true"` nor `1` was ever a substitute - the apiserver
  // refuses both for a `bool` field.
  //
  // ⚠ ***THE FIX ALSO GAVE `Resume("false")` AND `Resume("true")` MEANING***,
  // which is where the interesting part landed: a backstop-only park is now
  // SAYABLE. `quiesce('')` is refused because "a park must say what would change
  // its mind", and until booleans parsed an empty resume meant both "I forgot"
  // and "nothing but time will". See reconcilehost.Holds.

  /// Declare that `path`'s `field` should equal `value`.
  ///
  /// Returns nothing, like every other effect: an obligation is DECLARED here
  /// and performed by radiant, so there is no result a guest could branch on
  /// and no way to make a program's conclusion depend on a write succeeding.
  ensure: func(path: string, field: string, %value: value);

  /// One field of the body: a DOTTED path and a typed value.
  ///
  /// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  /// ***LIST ELEMENTS ARE `[N]`, NOT A BARE NUMERIC SEGMENT.***
  ///
  ///	spec.containers[0].name    an INDEX into a list
  ///	data.0                     a map KEY that happens to be "0"
  ///
  /// ⚠ BOTH ARE REAL AND THE AMBIGUITY IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL. Measured against
  /// the apiserver: `kubectl create configmap --from-literal=0=zero` yields
  /// `{"0":"zero"}`, so a numeric map key is legal. A bare `spec.x.0` therefore
  /// cannot be resolved without knowing the target's SCHEMA, which is knowledge
  /// no layer between the guest and the apply has - and guessing would mean a
  /// ConfigMap key silently becoming a list index. Brackets remove the question
  /// rather than answering it, and match how these paths are written everywhere
  /// else (JSONPath, kubectl).
  ///
  /// ***WHY NOT A RECURSIVE VALUE, WHICH IS THE OBVIOUS DESIGN.*** A variant arm
  /// `items(list<value>)` would carry nesting directly and needs no path syntax
  /// at all. WIT refuses it - measured, not assumed:
  ///
  ///	error: failed to parse package: type `value` depends on itself
  ///
  /// The component model has no recursive types, so the shape has to be flat and
  /// the nesting has to live in the path. That is a constraint of the boundary
  /// rather than a preference.
  ///
  /// Indices must be DENSE FROM ZERO - `[0]`, `[1]`, ... - because a list has no
  /// holes and inventing a null to fill one would put a value in the object the
  /// guest never wrote. A gap is an error, not a padded list.
  /// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ///
  /// A key containing a literal dot is not expressible; that is the cost of the
  /// flat form and it is rare enough to name rather than solve.
  record field {
    %path: string,
    %value: value,
  }

  /// Declare that SEVERAL of `path`'s fields should hold the given values - as
  /// ONE apiserver write, so a reader can never observe the new value of one
  /// field beside the old value of another.
  ///
  /// ***`ensure` IS ONE FIELD PER OBLIGATION, AND OBLIGATIONS APPLY ONE AT A
  /// TIME.*** A step writing `data.v` and a `data.t` stamp as two `ensure`s
  /// produced a durable torn pair - 3 of 6 convergences observed with new `v`
  /// beside old `t` (overhead-bench, 2026-09-01, building an operator on this).
  /// Level-triggered re-derivation does not save it: the torn state stands until
  /// the next pass. The controller-runtime arm it was measured against had no
  /// such problem, because both fields were one PATCH.
  ///
  /// The body is lowered exactly as `create`'s is - dotted paths to a nested
  /// struct, keys SORTED - so two orderings of one write render identically. The
  /// expression is the obligation's identity; unsorted, dedup would see a new
  /// obligation every pass.
  ///
  /// ⚠ ***`spec.replicas` ON AN apps KIND IS REFUSED BY THE HOST, WITH THE
  /// REASON.*** That field is written through the `/scale` subresource for least
  /// privilege, and the apiserver has no write that is atomic across a
  /// subresource boundary - the refusal names a real limit. `ensure` for the
  /// count, `ensure-all` for the rest.
  ensure-all: func(path: string, fields: list<field>);
}

/// REMOVE AN OBJECT THE APERTURE ALLOWS.
///
/// ***ITS OWN INTERFACE, NOT A FLAG ON `ensure`.*** The interface is the grant,
/// so a program that may edit a ConfigMap's data must not thereby be able to
/// delete it. `internal/aperture/ensure.go` separates `IfaceDelete` for exactly
/// that reason and this is the half that was missing.
///
/// The commonest legitimate caller is a FINALIZER - tearing down what a program
/// created - which until now could express no cleanup at all beyond scaling a
/// workload to zero.
/// CREATE AN OBJECT THE APERTURE ALLOWS.
///
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// The third and last of the capability ids `internal/aperture/ensure.go` has
/// declared with no interface behind them - `IfaceCreate` conferred the `Create`
/// action, `spec.writes` bounded it, the applier performed it, and nothing could
/// call it.
///
/// # THE BODY IS A FLAT LIST OF DOTTED PATHS, NOT A JSON STRING
///
/// ***AND THAT IS A SECURITY DECISION, NOT AN ERGONOMIC ONE.*** A JSON object is
/// ALREADY valid struct-literal syntax in this grammar - measured: `Create(p,
/// {"data": {"mode": "fast"}})` parses - so the tempting implementation is for
/// trail to interpolate the guest's JSON verbatim. That is an injection surface.
/// A guest sending the body
///
///	{"a": 1}, Delete("/api/v1/namespaces/t/configmaps/victim")
///
/// produces an expression that PARSES, as a three-argument `Create`. It is
/// refused today only because effect symbols do not resolve in argument
/// position - "unresolved identifier: Delete" - which is defence by accident of
/// the symbol table rather than by design, and is exactly the kind of property
/// that stops being true.
///
/// So every token of the body is rendered by trail from TYPED data and no
/// guest-controlled text reaches the expression unquoted. The keys are dotted
/// paths (`metadata.labels.app`), which trail expands into the nested literal
/// the host's `structLiteral` consumes.
///
/// ⚠ ***KEYS MUST RENDER SORTED.*** `structLiteral` sorts them, the obligation
/// IS its own identity, and the ledger dedups on the bytes - so a body rendered
/// in insertion order would make an unchanged Create look new on every pass:
/// applied forever, retired never.
///
/// ⛔ ***NO ARRAYS, MEASURED.*** `Create(p, {"spec": {"containers": [{...}]}})`
/// DOES NOT PARSE - the grammar's `[` is postfix indexing (`labels["x"]`), not
/// an array literal, and there is no `null` either. So a ConfigMap, a Secret and
/// a simple CR are expressible and a Deployment is NOT. That is a real limit and
/// the remedy is the grammar, exactly as it was for booleans - said here rather
/// than discovered by whoever first tries to create a workload.
interface create {
  /// ***REUSES `ensure`'s VALUE RATHER THAN RESTATING IT.*** Two identical
  /// variants in two interfaces would be two facts that must agree, and the one
  /// that drifts is the one nothing fails on - this package has paid for that
  /// shape twice (HOST_PACKAGES, and the vendored WIT this file is copied into).
  // `field` lives in `ensure` since 2026-09-01, because `ensure-all` takes the
  // same list. A `use` re-exports it, so `create::field` is still a name.
  use ensure.{value, field};

  /// Declare that an object should exist at `path` with these fields.
  ///
  /// IDEMPOTENT BY CONTRACT: the applier treats AlreadyExists as success, so a
  /// program re-declaring a Create every pass - which a total step does, having
  /// no memory of the last one - is correct rather than an error to suppress.
  create: func(path: string, body: list<field>);
}

interface delete {
  /// Declare that the object at `path` should not exist.
  ///
  /// IDEMPOTENT BY CONTRACT: the applier treats NotFound as success, so a
  /// finalizer re-declaring a delete on every attempt - which it must, having no
  /// memory of the last one - is correct rather than an error to suppress.
  delete: func(path: string);
}

interface step {
  run: func() -> string;
}

/// THE DELETION PATH: what a program does while its Perseid is being removed.
///
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// engi, 2026-08-30: *"finalizers can be a different entrypoint"* - and that is
/// what makes finalizers safe to have at all here.
///
/// # WHY IT IS A SEPARATE EXPORT AND NOT A FLAG ON `step`
///
/// A finalizer BLOCKS DELETION until it is removed. A Perseid `step` PARKS by
/// design - that is the whole model - so a step that also finalized could park
/// while holding an object undeletable, and the two states are indistinguishable
/// from outside: "correctly waiting for the world to change" and "wedged, and
/// nothing can be deleted until somebody notices" look identical in
/// `status.waitingFor`.
///
/// Splitting them lets the two have DIFFERENT CONTRACTS, which is the point:
///
///	step      may park, may yield, may terminate. Unbounded by design.
///	finalize  MUST return. It cannot park - there is no `quiesce` in its
///	          outcome - and the host bounds it, because whatever it is waiting
///	          for cannot be worth an object nobody can delete.
///
/// # IT IS TOTAL, LIKE `step`, AND FOR A SHARPER REASON
///
/// It runs on a FRESH instance with no memory of any pass (`reconcile.rs` builds
/// a Store per call and drops it). That is deliberate and measured: `reactor-
/// probe` observed a resident store letting a guest counter climb 1 -> 2 across
/// calls, which makes ADR-0075's "a step is TOTAL" documented rather than
/// enforced. A finalizer is the WORST place to have carried state - it runs
/// while an object is stuck, possibly after a crash, possibly on another node.
///
/// # WHAT IT RETURNS
///
/// `done` -> the host removes the finalizer and the object goes.
/// `retry` -> the host will call again, bounded. The message is for an operator
///	reading the Perseid, because a finalizer that keeps retrying is the state
///	somebody has to be told about rather than left to discover.
///
/// ***THERE IS NO "FAIL" AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.*** A finalizer that could fail
/// permanently would leave the object undeletable with no path forward, which is
/// the exact outcome the split above exists to prevent. Retry-with-a-reason is
/// the honest encoding: either it eventually completes, or an operator sees why
/// it has not.
interface finalize {
  /// The outcome of one finalization attempt.
  variant outcome {
    /// Cleanup is complete; the finalizer may be removed.
    done,
    /// Not yet. The string is for an operator, not for control flow.
    retry(string),
  }

  run: func() -> outcome;
}

world perseid {
  import observe;
  import observe-cluster;
  // Offered by the world, GRANTED by spec.capabilities. Importing an interface
  // makes it callable; `radiant:reconcile/observe-secrets@0.1.0` in the manifest
  // is what decides whether the call is answered - see perseidrun.answerer.
  import observe-secrets;
  import observe-configmaps;
  import workloads;
  import status;
  import ensure;
  import delete;
  import create;
  export step;
}

/// A Perseid that also cleans up after itself.
///
/// SEPARATE FROM `perseid` so the export stays OPTIONAL: a program with nothing
/// to clean up exports `step` alone and the host never calls a finalizer it does
/// not have. Making `finalize` part of the base world would force every existing
/// component to grow an entrypoint it does not need, and a stub that returns
/// `done` immediately is a finalizer that LOOKS like cleanup and is not.
world perseid-finalizing {
  import observe;
  import observe-cluster;
  // Offered by the world, GRANTED by spec.capabilities. Importing an interface
  // makes it callable; `radiant:reconcile/observe-secrets@0.1.0` in the manifest
  // is what decides whether the call is answered - see perseidrun.answerer.
  import observe-secrets;
  import observe-configmaps;
  import workloads;
  import status;
  import ensure;
  import delete;
  import create;
  export step;
  export finalize;
}

/// A world that SUPPLIES the step's capabilities.
///
/// SUPERSEDED MECHANISM - DO NOT BUILD FROM THIS SENTENCE. The description below
/// says composing a `perseid` with a `provider` (wac plug) yields a component
/// with no reconcile imports left. That is the FUSED-PROVIDER design, and
/// ADR-0082 replaced it: trail links `observe`/`emit` IN-PROCESS and radiant
/// implements them, so there is no provider component to fuse.
///
/// TRUE OF trail AS OF 8fd112158 (2026-08-18), AND THIS PARAGRAPH SAID THE
/// OPPOSITE FOR A DAY. It is rewritten rather than deleted because the sequence
/// is the useful part: the sentence above was written as DESIGN, read as
/// BEHAVIOUR, and was measured false — zero `add_to_linker` for reconcile, zero
/// bindgen worlds for it — at a moment when someone was about to add
/// `radiant:reconcile` to HOST_PACKAGES on the strength of it. Then the linker
/// landed and made it true, which is the only reason this now reads as an
/// ordinary description.
///
///     2026-08-18  measured: 0 add_to_linker for reconcile
///     8fd112158   2 — observe and emit, on BOTH p3 paths
///
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// ***THIS PARAGRAPH SAID "STILL NOT IN HOST_PACKAGES" AND ENDED "LIST IT WHEN
/// RADIANT SERVES". IT WAS LISTED ON 2026-08-21. THE SENTENCE OUTLIVED ITS OWN
/// CONDITION BY FOUR DAYS*** (corrected 2026-08-25 by trail-main, who READ this
/// paragraph the same day, believed it, and propagated it into a committed test
/// comment, a commit message and a peer report before radiant-main caught it.
/// `plug.rs` was one grep away throughout).
///
/// The release was a measurement, not a judgement: `reconcile_resolved_total`
/// went above zero on a live node — 119 on engix99 — so radiant demonstrably
/// SERVES `observe` and `workloads`.
///
/// ***AND A NUMBER THAT ANSWERED ONE DOOR READ AS ANSWERING THE SENTENCE, WHICH
/// DESCRIBED TWO*** (radiant-main). 119 resolutions establish that radiant
/// answers what it is asked. They say nothing about whether every interface in
/// the package EXISTS or is SERVED — `emit` was already deleted when the entry
/// landed, and `status` was added on 2026-08-25 with no serving half at all:
/// aperture has no `setCondition` action and `performs` has no status entry, so
/// every `status.set` is refused and the guest cannot see it, because `set`
/// returns nothing by contract.
///
/// ***SO THE ORIGINAL HAZARD IS BACK, ONE INTERFACE AT A TIME, AND NOTHING
/// CHECKS FOR IT.*** HOST_PACKAGES grants per PACKAGE while imports demand per
/// INTERFACE, so `hostpackagelinked_test.go` is satisfied by the package being
/// listed and is INVARIANT under adding an unserved interface to it. A Perseid
/// importing `status` is admitted against a host that cannot answer.
/// ***DO NOT delist the package to fix this*** — that breaks `observe` and
/// `workloads`, which are genuinely served. The missing thing is a way to say
/// "this package is satisfied EXCEPT for this interface", which does not exist.
/// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
///
/// `internal/trailop/hostpackagelinked_test.go` (3e2eb1e0c) makes that
/// distinguishable rather than trusting this comment: a package trail LINKS must
/// be in HOST_PACKAGES or carry a written reason it is not, so an oversight
/// (periapsis:host, 2026-08-15) cannot be mistaken for a decision (this one).
/// LIST IT WHEN RADIANT SERVES.
///
/// Two claims that were here and are now WRONG, kept visible because a stale
/// warning is worse than none — it is evidence somebody looked:
///
///   - "radiant refuses the Perseid for a declaration that is true" — FIXED by
///     0415362a5. ResolveImports diffs against imports UNION seamNeeds, so the
///     partition's own subtraction is stateable and a correct program is
///     admitted.
///   - "the unblocking work is trail's linker, not the list" — the linker
///     landed. The blocker MOVED: it is radiant SERVING observe/emit, plus the
///     per-call authorization ADR-0082 records as not existing today.
///
/// One correction to the original measurement, kept for the same reason: it said
/// "zero occurrences of perseid" and there is ONE — `Perseid` at main.rs:1282, a
/// comment. The grep was case-sensitive. It does not move the conclusion, and a
/// measurement that was wrong in a footnote is how one wrong in a premise gets
/// believed.
///
/// The ADR's title was corrected to "Radiant SERVES a Perseid's world" for this
/// reason; the filename still says "composes" only because renaming rots every
/// reference.
///
/// The world is kept because it still types the CONTRACT - what a supplier must
/// export - which is unchanged by who supplies it. What changed is the delivery
/// mechanism, not the shape.
///
/// Bannered 2026-08-17. It was listed in docs/perseid-adr-retraction-map.md as
/// "flagged in the ADR as needing a rewrite; not done" - and a rewrite the map
/// knows about but the FILE does not is exactly the failure that map exists to
/// record: the index is not the artifact.
///
/// Composing a `perseid` with a `provider` (wac plug) yields a component with no
/// reconcile imports left.
world provider {
  export observe;
  export observe-cluster;
  export workloads;
  export status;
}

/// A provider that supplies only PART of the contract. It exists so the
/// link-time failure can be demonstrated rather than asserted: composing a
/// step against this must FAIL, naming the capability nobody satisfies.
///
/// ***IT NOW OMITS TWO INTERFACES AND ITS SENTENCE ABOVE SAYS "THE CAPABILITY",
/// SINGULAR*** (trail-main, 2026-08-25, recording a consequence of adding
/// `status` rather than fixing it silently). Adding a third interface to
/// `perseid` widened this world's gap from one missing capability to two, so a
/// composition failure here can name either and the demonstration no longer
/// pins WHICH. Left as-is because nothing composes it today — the only
/// reference in the tree is a comment in
/// `examples/wasm/perseid-component/build.sh` — so changing it would be
/// adjusting a demonstration nobody runs on the strength of a guess about what
/// it should demonstrate.
///
/// If it is ever wired up, export `status` here too: omitting exactly ONE
/// interface is what makes the error message a measurement instead of a
/// coin-flip, and that property is the whole reason this world exists.
world partial-provider {
  export observe;
}

/// Drives a step: calls `run` repeatedly and acts on the outcome. Composed with
/// a capability-closed step (one already plugged to a provider), this is the
/// loop running INSIDE the component model — the execution half of the join.
world driver {
  include wasi:cli/command@0.3.0;
  import step;
}

/// The relay's world: EXACTLY what the code imports, and nothing else.
///
/// ***BUILDING AGAINST `world perseid` MAKES THE ARTIFACT DEMAND CAPABILITIES THE
/// CODE NEVER CALLS*** — measured: observe-cluster, observe-secrets, workloads,
/// status, delete and create, none of which this program calls. Admission is
/// demand MINUS grant, so the grant would have to cover reading every Secret in
/// the namespace and creating and deleting objects, for a program that copies one
/// string between two ConfigMaps.
///
/// `observe-configmaps` is NOT imported and its absence is correct: it is a
/// capability, not a call — the interface is deliberately empty, a ConfigMap is
/// read through `observe.get` by path, and the host gates that per kind. It is
/// named in spec.capabilities instead, so this program's GRANT is a strict
/// superset of its DEMAND. Admission refuses demand the grant does not cover,
/// never the reverse.
///
/// `wasi:cli/environment` is declared because the step reads spec.config through
/// `process.env`, and dwarf's shim binds against what the WORLD declares — not
/// against whatever the JS runtime happens to drag into the import section. The
/// artifact carried 0.2.12 before this line existed and the step still trapped.
world relay {
  import observe;
  import ensure;
  import wasi:cli/environment@0.3.0;
  export step;
}
